{
  "//": "STACKBLITZ DOES NOT CURRENTLY WORK — configured to get as far as it can, so that if the upstream gap closes it should simply start. It boots via npm because WebContainer's bundled pnpm 8.15.6 rejects pnpm-workspace.yaml and no pnpm it can execute is new enough for this repo; without that it fails immediately. The step order matters: the first install passes --ignore-scripts so the `nuxt prepare` postinstall does not run before a SQLite driver exists (it would have @nuxt/content install sqlite3 via nypm, which reads packageManager, runs `pnpm add sqlite3`, and dies with ERR_PNPM_ADDING_TO_ROOT); sqlite3 is then installed --no-save, with scripts, so its prebuilt binary is fetched and Content -- which selects that connector by itself in a WebContainer -- finds it already present. npm_config_sqlite3_binary_host_mirror is required because prebuild-install pulls from GitHub releases, unreachable from WebContainer and reported only as a warning, so sqlite3 otherwise installs silently with no binding. --legacy-peer-deps covers three unmet peers npm rejects and pnpm tolerates, and NODE_ENV is set because WebContainer defaults it to production, which would skip devDependencies nuxt.config.ts needs. What still blocks it is WebContainer refusing native addons: sqlite3 is one, so it fails with ERR_DLOPEN_DISABLED even with the binary in place. Nothing here can fix that. Note npm cannot read pnpm-lock.yaml, so this resolves fresh from package.json ranges — acceptable only while this cannot start at all, and something to solve before relying on it. Full diagnosis and retest: docs/setup-development-environment.md, section 'StackBlitz (does not currently work)'.",
  "installDependencies": false,
  "startCommand": "npm install --legacy-peer-deps --ignore-scripts && npm install --no-save sqlite3 && npm run dev",
  "env": {
    "NODE_ENV": "development",
    "npm_config_sqlite3_binary_host_mirror": "https://registry.npmmirror.com/-/binary/sqlite3"
  }
}
